1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:10,136
CI, the automation that builds and tests
every change, clones 3 GB for every job.

2
00:00:10,176 --> 00:00:14,481
A clone is your own copy of a
repository, made with git clone.

3
00:00:14,521 --> 00:00:19,596
Someone proposes --depth 1 everywhere,
and the pipeline gets much faster.

4
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:23,800
Your CTO asks one question before
approving it: what breaks?

5
00:00:23,840 --> 00:00:25,622
Make your guess, out loud.

6
00:00:30,462 --> 00:00:35,429
The uncomfortable answer from Chapter
26: history questions break, silently.

7
00:00:35,469 --> 00:00:40,897
Versions, blame, merge bases. The commands
exit with status 0 and print a plausible

8
00:00:40,937 --> 00:00:45,442
answer that is wrong. And a partial
clone is usually what was wanted.

9
00:00:45,482 --> 00:00:49,065
Hold on to that word, silently.
You'll watch it happen.

10
00:00:50,905 --> 00:00:54,750
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:54,790 --> 00:00:59,463
This is the longest video of the part,
and it has one thread: a fetch is a

12
00:00:59,503 --> 00:01:04,304
conversation, and every special clone
is one more line in that conversation.

13
00:01:04,344 --> 00:01:08,507
A fetch is how a clone downloads
what the server has and it lacks.

14
00:01:08,547 --> 00:01:13,666
Once you see the line, you can reason about
what the clone holds and what it will

15
00:01:13,706 --> 00:01:14,366
cost later.

16
00:01:14,706 --> 00:01:23,721
Four replays from labs/ch26:
fetch-conversation.sh, shallow-limits.sh,

17
00:01:23,761 --> 00:01:33,423
partial-clone.sh and clone-shapes.sh.
The server is a bare copy of orbit, a

18
00:01:33,463 --> 00:01:44,030
repository without a working tree, reached
through a file:// URL, so that Git uses

19
00:01:44,070 --> 00:01:46,487
its real transport.

20
00:01:46,827 --> 00:01:52,666
Labels: git clone with --depth or --filter,
git fetch --unshallow and git backfill

21
00:01:52,706 --> 00:01:56,537
are 🟢 SAFE. They add objects
and read from the remote.

22
00:01:56,577 --> 00:02:01,341
A shallow or partial clone holds
fewer objects, not different ones.

23
00:02:03,181 --> 00:02:04,593
After this video you can:

24
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:10,368
Describe a fetch as a conversation of
wants and haves and say where --depth and

25
00:02:10,408 --> 00:02:11,615
--filter enter it.

26
00:02:11,955 --> 00:02:13,658
Say what a shallow, a blobless, a treeless
and a single-branch clone each hold.

27
00:02:13,998 --> 00:02:17,917
Name the commands that give wrong or
failing answers in a shallow clone.

28
00:02:18,257 --> 00:02:22,230
Explain on-demand fetching in a
partial clone and what fails offline.

29
00:02:22,570 --> 00:02:26,305
Choose a clone shape for a developer,
for CI and for a one-off build.

30
00:02:28,145 --> 00:02:32,657
The fetch conversation. In one sentence:
a fetch is a short dialogue in which the

31
00:02:32,697 --> 00:02:37,153
server advertises what it has, the client
says what it wants and what it already

32
00:02:37,193 --> 00:02:40,524
has, and the server answers with
one pack of the difference.

33
00:02:40,564 --> 00:02:45,020
On screen is today's example: a clone
before a fetch, and after it, with two new

34
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:45,470
commits.

35
00:02:45,810 --> 00:02:49,126
Four steps are enough to reason
about every clone shape.

36
00:02:49,466 --> 00:02:54,916
One, advertise. The server names the protocol
version and its capabilities, and the

37
00:02:54,956 --> 00:02:59,678
client asks for refs, the names such
as branches and tags, with ls-refs.

38
00:03:00,018 --> 00:03:05,170
Two, want. The client names the tips it
wants, the newest commits of those branches.

39
00:03:05,210 --> 00:03:10,239
Three, have. The client names commits it
has, and the server acknowledges those it

40
00:03:10,279 --> 00:03:14,071
knows. This negotiation finds
the common boundary. Four, pack.

41
00:03:14,111 --> 00:03:19,202
The server enumerates the objects on its
side of the boundary and sends them as one

42
00:03:19,242 --> 00:03:22,169
packfile, a single file
that holds many objects.

43
00:03:22,509 --> 00:03:26,122
A clone has nothing to offer,
so it sends wants and done.

44
00:03:26,162 --> 00:03:31,313
Each special clone adds one line. deepen
1 makes the server stop after one commit

45
00:03:31,353 --> 00:03:36,440
per tip. filter blob:none makes the server
leave out objects the filter rejects.

46
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:41,824
Both are decisions of the server's enumeration.
The client receives an ordinary pack

47
00:03:41,864 --> 00:03:42,529
either way.

48
00:03:42,869 --> 00:03:47,578
Shallow clones. In one sentence: a shallow
clone contains the commits within a given

49
00:03:47,618 --> 00:03:52,326
distance of the requested tips and a file
that tells Git to pretend the history ends

50
00:03:52,366 --> 00:03:52,665
there.

51
00:03:53,005 --> 00:03:56,876
Now precisely: git clone
--depth <n> sends deepen <n>.

52
00:03:56,916 --> 00:04:02,598
The server sends the commits within that
distance with their complete trees and

53
00:04:02,638 --> 00:04:08,609
blobs. The client writes the IDs of the
boundary commits into $GIT_DIR/shallow, and

54
00:04:08,649 --> 00:04:12,303
Git treats those commits
as if they had no parents.

55
00:04:12,343 --> 00:04:17,518
Two side effects matter. --depth
implies --single-branch unless you give

56
00:04:17,558 --> 00:04:22,805
--no-single-branch. And tags that point
behind the boundary don't arrive.

57
00:04:22,845 --> 00:04:28,888
Related options: --shallow-since and
--shallow-exclude on clone and fetch, git fetch

58
00:04:28,928 --> 00:04:31,496
--deepen, and git fetch --unshallow.

59
00:04:31,836 --> 00:04:36,619
Partial clones. In one sentence: a partial
clone has all of the history's structure

60
00:04:36,659 --> 00:04:41,385
and leaves out objects of a chosen kind,
which a designated remote has promised to

61
00:04:41,425 --> 00:04:43,361
deliver when a command needs them.

62
00:04:43,701 --> 00:04:47,897
git clone --filter=<spec> sends
filter <spec>. Three forms.

63
00:04:47,937 --> 00:04:53,353
blob:none, called blobless: all commits
and trees, no blobs except those the

64
00:04:53,393 --> 00:04:59,311
checkout needs. A blob is the content of
one file, and a tree is the listing of one

65
00:04:59,351 --> 00:05:05,270
directory. tree:0, called treeless: all
commits, no trees or blobs except those the

66
00:05:05,310 --> 00:05:10,223
checkout needs. And blob:limit=<n>:
omit blobs of at least that size.

67
00:05:10,563 --> 00:05:14,453
Inside .git, the clone records
remote.<name>.promisor=true and

68
00:05:14,493 --> 00:05:19,778
remote.<name>.partialclonefilter. Packs from
that remote get a .promisor file beside

69
00:05:19,818 --> 00:05:24,658
them, and an object that is missing but
referenced from such a pack counts as

70
00:05:24,698 --> 00:05:29,856
promised, not as corruption. When a command
needs a missing object, Git fetches it

71
00:05:29,896 --> 00:05:32,708
from the promisor remote,
without asking you.

72
00:05:33,048 --> 00:05:36,440
Whether a filter is honoured
is the server's decision.

73
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:41,587
GitHub supports partial clone. A self-hosted
server needs uploadpack.allowFilter.

74
00:05:41,927 --> 00:05:49,989
--single-branch is the fourth shape. It
narrows the refspec, the rule that says

75
00:05:50,029 --> 00:05:58,605
which branches a fetch brings, to one branch.
It limits history by reachability, not

76
00:05:58,645 --> 00:05:59,528
by depth.

77
00:05:59,868 --> 00:06:04,102
Failure modes, one per shape. Shallow:
wrong answers without a warning.

78
00:06:04,142 --> 00:06:09,098
Partial: slowness when a command fetches
objects one request at a time, and failure

79
00:06:09,138 --> 00:06:13,853
when the promisor remote can't be reached.
Single-branch: other branches aren't

80
00:06:13,893 --> 00:06:17,164
there, and the refspec stays
narrow until you widen it.

81
00:06:17,504 --> 00:06:22,680
When to use which is the table at the
end of section 26.13, and we'll end the

82
00:06:22,720 --> 00:06:24,035
demonstration on it.

83
00:06:25,875 --> 00:06:30,084
Two analogies from the textbook, one
for each kind of incomplete clone.

84
00:06:30,424 --> 00:06:33,781
A shallow clone is the last
page of a ledger, photocopied.

85
00:06:33,821 --> 00:06:38,641
It's enough to see today's balance and
useless for an audit, and if you forget that

86
00:06:38,681 --> 00:06:43,384
it's a photocopy of one page you'll conclude
that the business started yesterday.

87
00:06:43,424 --> 00:06:48,010
The analogy breaks in one direction:
unlike a photocopy, a shallow clone can be

88
00:06:48,050 --> 00:06:48,889
deepened later.

89
00:06:49,229 --> 00:06:53,015
A partial clone is a book whose
footnotes are kept at the publisher.

90
00:06:53,055 --> 00:06:57,685
The text is complete, every footnote number
is there, and when you look one up it's

91
00:06:57,725 --> 00:07:02,355
sent to you and stays in your copy. Read
offline, the footnotes you never looked up

92
00:07:02,395 --> 00:07:06,744
are unavailable. The analogy breaks at
who looks things up: here it is any Git

93
00:07:06,784 --> 00:07:08,319
command, without asking you.

94
00:07:08,659 --> 00:07:13,087
The difference between the two is the
difference between not knowing and knowing

95
00:07:13,127 --> 00:07:16,550
what you lack. A shallow clone
has been told the history ends.

96
00:07:16,590 --> 00:07:20,571
A partial clone knows exactly which
objects exist and where to get them.

97
00:07:22,411 --> 00:07:27,842
Read the commits column first. Full and
both partial clones have all 96 commits.

98
00:07:27,882 --> 00:07:32,628
Single-branch has 94: the two commits
of the other branch are missing.

99
00:07:32,668 --> 00:07:33,722
Depth 1 has one.

100
00:07:34,062 --> 00:07:37,504
Now the blobs column.
Thirty-three appears three times.

101
00:07:37,544 --> 00:07:40,795
Thirty-three is the number
of files in the checkout.

102
00:07:40,835 --> 00:07:46,049
The shallow clone and both partial clones
hold exactly one snapshot's worth of file

103
00:07:46,089 --> 00:07:46,555
content.

104
00:07:46,895 --> 00:07:50,384
So what separates --depth
1 from --filter=blob:none?

105
00:07:50,424 --> 00:07:56,085
The commits and the trees. The blobless
clone has the whole structure of history and

106
00:07:56,125 --> 00:07:59,750
can answer history questions.
The shallow clone can't.

107
00:07:59,790 --> 00:08:05,316
And the last column says how each treats
what it lacks: the shallow clone pretends

108
00:08:05,356 --> 00:08:08,845
it doesn't exist. The partial
clones hold a promise.

109
00:08:13,185 --> 00:08:18,653
Step one, advertise. Protocol version 2.
The line fetch=shallow wait-for-done filter

110
00:08:18,693 --> 00:08:22,654
is the server saying which extras
its fetch command supports.

111
00:08:22,694 --> 00:08:27,309
filter appears only because the lab
server sets uploadpack.allowFilter.

112
00:08:27,649 --> 00:08:31,457
A full clone. How many have
lines does the client send?

113
00:08:31,497 --> 00:08:33,135
Say a number. I'll wait.

114
00:08:37,975 --> 00:08:42,347
Eight wants, one per ref. Zero
haves: a clone has nothing to offer.

115
00:08:42,387 --> 00:08:44,718
The answer is one section, the pack.

116
00:08:45,058 --> 00:08:49,728
Here are the two extra lines. deepen 1,
and the answer begins with a shallow-info

117
00:08:49,768 --> 00:08:53,799
section naming the commit where
history was cut. And filter blob:none.

118
00:08:54,139 --> 00:08:57,244
An ordinary fetch, later.
One wanted tip. Five haves.

119
00:08:57,284 --> 00:09:02,050
The server acknowledges them, says ready,
and sends a pack of the two new commits

120
00:09:02,090 --> 00:09:03,652
with their trees and blobs.

121
00:09:03,992 --> 00:09:09,229
On the graph, the two new commits have
arrived, and origin/main now names 9ea7ebd.

122
00:09:17,269 --> 00:09:21,446
The saving is the two commits of the
other branch with their trees and blobs.

123
00:09:21,486 --> 00:09:26,046
It pays off when a repository has long-lived
branches with large unshared histories,

124
00:09:26,086 --> 00:09:27,579
and hardly at all otherwise.

125
00:09:27,919 --> 00:09:32,658
One commit, its 20 trees and 33
blobs: one complete snapshot.

126
00:09:32,698 --> 00:09:34,226
And one tag of five.

127
00:09:37,065 --> 00:09:42,400
A quick quiz. The commit object of HEAD:
does it still have a parent header, yes or

128
00:09:42,440 --> 00:09:43,436
no? Your answer?

129
00:09:48,276 --> 00:09:53,936
It does: it names d259a34. An object
can't be altered without changing its ID.

130
00:09:53,976 --> 00:09:59,928
What changed is Git's reading: the ID in
.git/shallow makes every walk stop there,

131
00:09:59,968 --> 00:10:03,436
%p prints no parent, and
HEAD~1 doesn't resolve.

132
00:10:03,776 --> 00:10:09,185
Try it now, thirty seconds, in a repository
in the lab shell: run git rev-parse

133
00:10:09,225 --> 00:10:11,599
--is-shallow-repository. I'll wait.

134
00:10:16,439 --> 00:10:21,501
A repository you created yourself prints
false. In a CI checkout, that one word is

135
00:10:21,541 --> 00:10:23,181
the first thing to look at.

136
00:10:23,521 --> 00:10:28,787
Before each answer appears, say what the
full repository would answer: 94 commits,

137
00:10:28,827 --> 00:10:33,835
13 commits on the ranker. What will this
clone say, and with what exit status?

138
00:10:33,875 --> 00:10:34,482
I'll wait.

139
00:10:39,322 --> 00:10:42,477
This is the danger, and it's
the silently of the hook.

140
00:10:42,517 --> 00:10:47,447
Every command exits with status 0 and a
plausible answer: one commit in history, one

141
00:10:47,487 --> 00:10:52,299
commit that ever touched the ranker, every
line of the file blamed on the boundary

142
00:10:52,339 --> 00:10:56,915
commit, which the caret marks, and one
author. Only git describe fails loudly,

143
00:10:56,955 --> 00:11:01,826
because the gateway tags lie behind the
boundary. And git commit-graph write writes

144
00:11:01,866 --> 00:11:03,424
nothing, without a message.

145
00:11:15,763 --> 00:11:21,606
--deepen=5 moved the boundary five commits
back. --unshallow removed it and brought

146
00:11:21,646 --> 00:11:27,560
the tags: 94 commits, and git describe
answers. One thing didn't change: the refspec

147
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:33,230
is still the narrow one that --depth
implied. The clone still follows main only.

148
00:11:33,570 --> 00:11:39,869
All 96 commits, all 284 trees, and 33 blobs.
git cat-file warns that it lists only

149
00:11:39,909 --> 00:11:40,951
what is local.

150
00:11:43,791 --> 00:11:49,270
Eighty-nine objects that the history refers
to are absent, and git fsck exits 0.

151
00:11:49,310 --> 00:11:53,546
Git doesn't call that damage.
The packs carry .promisor files.

152
00:11:53,886 --> 00:11:59,068
A quick quiz. Three commands: git show of
an old file version, git log on a path,

153
00:11:59,108 --> 00:12:03,388
git diff --stat. Which of them
contact the server? Say it out loud.

154
00:12:08,228 --> 00:12:13,278
git show of an old version triggered a git
fetch for one object, with negotiation

155
00:12:13,318 --> 00:12:18,432
switched off. Commands that read only
commits and trees, git log on a path and git

156
00:12:18,472 --> 00:12:22,643
diff --name-status, fetched nothing:
the pack count stays at three.

157
00:12:22,683 --> 00:12:26,665
git diff --stat needed contents
and fetched them in one request.

158
00:12:27,005 --> 00:12:32,287
This is the cost. git log -p on one file
made 11 separate requests and git blame

159
00:12:32,327 --> 00:12:37,609
made 12, each a round trip to the server,
and the repository now holds 27 packs.

160
00:12:37,649 --> 00:12:42,930
The manual of git backfill describes it
in the same terms: such commands "become

161
00:12:42,970 --> 00:12:47,587
very slow as they download the missing
blobs in single-blob requests".

162
00:12:56,427 --> 00:13:00,161
git backfill is experimental
and needs Git 2.49 or later.

163
00:13:00,201 --> 00:13:03,803
It fetched everything reachable
from HEAD in one batch.

164
00:13:03,843 --> 00:13:07,577
The two objects still missing
belong to the other branch.

165
00:13:07,617 --> 00:13:11,285
Maintenance then merged 28
packs into one promisor pack.

166
00:13:11,625 --> 00:13:13,001
Now take the server away.

167
00:13:13,341 --> 00:13:18,440
The server is unreachable. Does git log
work? Does git switch to a branch whose

168
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:23,774
files were never fetched? And if it fails,
in what state does it leave the working

169
00:13:23,814 --> 00:13:26,312
tree? Make your predictions. I'll wait.

170
00:13:37,152 --> 00:13:42,088
Everything local works. Anything that
needs a missing blob fails with "could not

171
00:13:42,128 --> 00:13:47,314
fetch ... from promisor remote". git switch
failed before touching the working tree:

172
00:13:47,354 --> 00:13:50,175
git status afterwards
still shows main, clean.

173
00:13:50,515 --> 00:13:56,065
Treeless: only the 20 trees of the checkout
are present, so a walk that looks at

174
00:13:56,105 --> 00:14:01,655
paths must fetch trees. One git log on a
path sent 93 requests and left 96 packs

175
00:14:01,695 --> 00:14:02,144
behind.

176
00:14:05,484 --> 00:14:11,014
Two traps. A local path makes git clone
copy files, so the filter is ignored.

177
00:14:11,054 --> 00:14:16,947
Use a file:// URL. And a server without
uploadpack.allowFilter ignores the filter.

178
00:14:16,987 --> 00:14:22,807
In both cases you get a warning, a complete
clone, and a repository that is still

179
00:14:22,847 --> 00:14:24,398
configured as partial.

180
00:14:24,738 --> 00:14:29,837
A developer on a large repository: blobless,
with sparse-checkout if the tree is

181
00:14:29,877 --> 00:14:34,076
wide. A CI job that builds one
snapshot and is discarded: shallow.

182
00:14:34,116 --> 00:14:39,408
A CI job that computes versions, changelogs
or affected projects: blobless, or full

183
00:14:39,448 --> 00:14:44,739
depth. A mirror, a backup, a server: full.
A machine without a route to the server:

184
00:14:44,779 --> 00:14:47,501
full, or a bundle, which
is the next video.

185
00:14:49,341 --> 00:14:50,989
Five mistakes to watch for.

186
00:14:51,329 --> 00:14:55,473
Using --depth 1 for a job that
computes a version or a changelog.

187
00:14:55,513 --> 00:15:00,494
Root cause: the job needed history and
was given a snapshot; walks stop at the

188
00:15:00,534 --> 00:15:02,683
boundary and report what they saw.

189
00:15:03,023 --> 00:15:08,606
Expecting all branches and tags after
--depth. Root cause: --depth implies

190
00:15:08,646 --> 00:15:13,165
--single-branch, and tags behind
the boundary do not arrive.

191
00:15:13,505 --> 00:15:18,893
Running git log -p or git blame across
history in a fresh blobless clone and blaming

192
00:15:18,933 --> 00:15:24,321
the network. Root cause: these commands
fetch missing blobs in single-blob requests.

193
00:15:24,661 --> 00:15:29,592
Using a treeless clone for development.
Root cause: any walk that looks at paths

194
00:15:29,632 --> 00:15:32,016
must fetch trees, one commit at a time.

195
00:15:32,356 --> 00:15:38,513
Testing a filter with a local path. Root
cause: the local transport copies files and

196
00:15:38,553 --> 00:15:42,792
ignores the filter; a file://
URL uses the real transport.

197
00:15:44,632 --> 00:15:50,109
Now, out of the lab. A backend team's release
job tags builds with git describe and

198
00:15:50,149 --> 00:15:55,294
generates a changelog from git log. After
the switch to --depth 1, the version

199
00:15:55,334 --> 00:15:59,283
string is empty on some days
and the changelog has one line.

200
00:15:59,323 --> 00:16:04,800
On laptops the same commands are right. The
diagnosis is one command: git rev-parse

201
00:16:04,840 --> 00:16:07,193
--is-shallow-repository prints true.

202
00:16:07,533 --> 00:16:12,352
Their fix follows the table. Jobs that
build one snapshot and are thrown away stay

203
00:16:12,392 --> 00:16:17,330
shallow. The release job, which computes
versions and changelogs, becomes a blobless

204
00:16:17,370 --> 00:16:22,248
clone. That matches GitHub's own guidance,
which the textbook cites: shallow clones

205
00:16:22,288 --> 00:16:26,692
belong in builds that are thrown away,
because later fetches into a shallow

206
00:16:26,732 --> 00:16:31,611
repository are expensive for the server and
history commands break; blobless clones

207
00:16:31,651 --> 00:16:36,411
are recommended for developers, and treeless
clones only for builds that need the

208
00:16:36,451 --> 00:16:38,307
commit history and nothing else.

209
00:16:38,647 --> 00:16:43,811
Note also from the chapter that actions/checkout
produces a shallow, tagless clone

210
00:16:43,851 --> 00:16:44,509
by default.

211
00:16:46,349 --> 00:16:51,958
Your turn. Do Lab 18.1, "Clone one repository
three ways and compare what arrived",

